CON_0147	PAT_0031	rda_graph:PAT_0031	RDA National PID Strategies Guide and Checklist		https://zenodo.org/records/8431975		https://www.rd-alliance.org/group_output/rda-national-pid-strategies-guide-and-checklist/		DOI	https://doi.org/10.15497/RDA00091	2023-09-18	The National PID Strategies Working Group was endorsed to explore how Persistent Identifiers (PIDs) form part of national policy and research infrastructure implementation frameworks. The Group recognises that there are systemic and network benefits from widespread and consistent PID adoption including financial and time savings benefits. Research sector stakeholders including funders, government agencies, and national research communities have created PID consortia or policies (including mandates) in pursuit of these benefits. At the establishment of the WG, National PID Strategies were beginning to emerge in the UK, Australia, the Netherlands, and Canada as a pathway to realising these benefits and an international conversation felt needed. RDA provided an umbrella for discussion and alignment between the strategies, refinement of the value proposition and sharing practical development pathways to a national PID strategy.	eng	publication-other	info:eu-repo/semantics/other		\N	\N	\N	\N
CON_0177	PAT_0035	rda_graph:PAT_0035	Report prepared at Plenary 3		https://www.rd-alliance.org/group_output/report-prepared-at-plenary-3/						2016-06-09	The Interest Group on Structural Biology (SBIG hereafter) convened at the RDA Third Plenary conference on March 27th, 2014. The agenda and presentations from the two sessions of the meeting are available from the SBIG web page within the RDA site (https://rd-alliance.org/groups/structural-biology-ig/wiki/plenary-3-structural-biology-ig-session.html).\nIn addition to reviewing various aspects of the state-of-art of data management in structural biology (SB), this meeting provided also an opportunity to establish links with the Interest Group on “Research data needs of the Photon and Neutron Science community”, for which biology is a discipline of high relevance.\n	eng	publication-report	info:eu-repo/semantics/report		\N	\N	\N	\N
