CON_0108	PID_0081	rda_graph:PID_0081	Operationalizing and evaluating the FAIRness concept for a good quality of data sharing in Research: the RDA-SHARC-IG (SHAring Rewards and Credit Interest Group		https://zenodo.org/records/1745374				DOI	https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.1745373	2018-11-11	The RDA-SHARC (SHAring Reward & Credit) interest group is an interdisciplinary volunteer member-based group set up as part of RDA (Research Data Alliance) to unpack and improve crediting and rewarding mechanisms in the sharing process throughout the data life cycle. Background and objectives of this group are reported here. Notably, one of the objectives is to promote the inclusion of data sharing activities in the research (& researchers) assessment scheme at national and European levels. To this aim, the RDA-SHARC-IG is developing two assessment grids using criteria to establish if data are compliant to the F.A.I.R principles (findable /accessible / interoperable / reusable) based on previous works on FAIR data management (Reymonet et al., 2018; Wilkinson et al., 2018; and E.U.Guidelines*): 1/ The self-assessment grid to be used by a scientist as a ‘checklist’ to identify her/his own activities and to pinpoint the hurdles that hinder efficient sharing and reuse of his/her data by all potential users. 2/ The two-level grid (quick/extensive) to be used by the evaluator to assess the quality of the researcher/scientist sharing practice, over a given period, taking into account the means & support available over that period. Assessment criteria are classified according their importance with regards to FAIRness (essential / recommended / desirable) meanwhile good practices are recommended for critical steps. To implement a highly fair assessment of the sharing process, appropriate criteria must be selected in order to design optimal generic assessment grids. This process requires participation, time and input from volunteer scientists data producers/users from various fields.	eng	publication-poster	info:eu-repo/semantics/poster		\N	\N	\N	\N
CON_0110	PID_0082	rda_graph:PID_0082	Persistent identifiers: Consolidated assertions		https://zenodo.org/records/1116189		https://www.rd-alliance.org/group_output/persistent-identifiers-consolidated-assertions/		DOI	https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.1116189	2017-12-14	Experts from 47 European research infrastructure initiatives and ERICs have agreed on a set of assertions about the nature, the creation and the usage of Persistent Identifiers (PIDs). This work was done in close synchronisation with the RDA Data Fabric Interest Group (DFIG) ensuring a global validation of the assertions. The intention of this cross-disciplinary report is to overcome still existing confusions about PIDs and the lack of detail knowledge in many disciplines. It is not meant to produce yet another comprehensive document on PIDs, but to identify agreements across documents that have been suggested to be included by experts. With this document GEDE (Group of European Data Experts in RDA Europe) is happy to help demystify PIDs, overcome confusion and create bridges between the various disciplines.	eng	publication-report	info:eu-repo/semantics/report	https://www.rd-alliance.org/sites/default/files/Card%20RDA_Persistent%20identifiers_%20Consolidated%20assertions_single%2028_0.pdf	\N	\N	\N	\N
CON_0111	PID_0083	rda_graph:PID_0083	Persisting globally FAIR access in an CAREfully identifiable manner		https://zenodo.org/records/4708010		https://www.rd-alliance.org/rdas-17th-plenary-meeting-posters-0	https://archive.rd-alliance.org/rdas-17th-plenary-meeting-posters-0	DOI	https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4708009	2021-04-21	Networks of information are at the heart of ensuring fair and equitable access to scholarly information and the dissemination of works across an evolving landscape of national and international policy, platforms and people. Ensuring that works are registered in universally recognised ways allows them to participate as first class members of these global open knowledge environments without fear of bias or differentiation. The equality of recognition, discovery and access is fundamental to driving success across borders and regions. The principles of FAIRness – that works and the underlying infrastructure and metadata encourage: Findable, Accessible, Interoperable and Reusable information -- are key enablers to be embedded in any successful approach.\n\nAcross regions, where access to technical hardware and platforms may be difficult (such as Africa, SouthEast Asia, Ibero-America), it is fairly common to see a “head down”| mobile data economy emerge with feature phones and laptops, rather than desktop, being the primary means of interactions with online resources, and the high end connectivity, compute power and visualisation capability largely available in urban centres and institutional settings.\n\nThis means that truly inclusive global policy and practice must take account of all the varying restrictions and opportunities these differences may present. Without established practice there is a place to move directly to overlay journals and data repositories without embedding failed gouging closed publishing economies with 30-40% profit margins. Without established infrastructure, there is an opportunity to take learning and lived experience on deploying assets during the pandemic in areas without Wi-Fi and how agreements were negotiated with network providers on free access to learning materials to apply generally to building resiliency into learning infrastructure informed by unreliable grid and network infrastructure make delivery unpredictable (just as the internet grew out of resiliency planning). At the same time, in acknowledgement that the FAIR Principle do not articulate all relevant data cases and concerns, the CARE Principles (Collective, Authority to control, Responsibility, Ethics) are also increasingly embedding concepts of data stewardship, innovative and self-determination among indigenous data-holding communities. FAIR and CARE are approaching a number of similar issues – around individual vs common good, sharing, and power differentials. This provides an opportunity to discuss potential tensions using community-developed frameworks. There is also a place to look at interventions and pan-national support to counter instability caused by local policy reliant on patronage and the current political landscape with no persistence beyond an individual(‘s) administration. Best practices for interconnectivity developed on the national level will be comparable and inform other regional and national initiatives to utilize PID-based infrastructure on their own more efficiently.\n\nIn short, a FAIR approach to data and the wider scholarly infrastructure not only builds a more equitable global open science community and practice, but also allows all of us to recognise and build on the valuable experience and opportunities offered.	eng	publication-poster	info:eu-repo/semantics/poster		\N	\N	\N	\N
CON_0112	PID_0084	rda_graph:PID_0084	Personal Data Masking the Public Good in the GDPR: An Excursion into Personal Privacy and Private Goods		https://zenodo.org/records/7559238		https://www.rd-alliance.org/francis-p-crawley	https://archive.rd-alliance.org/francis-p-crawley	DOI	https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7559238	2023-01-22	This event was centered on ethical use of data and concerns on privacy versus common good. Ethics in the use of data was the theme of the Permanent Forum. In order to think about the ethical and legal aspects of data use, a new area of knowledge is constituted, involving philosophers, lawyers, economists, computer scientists, among other areas of knowledge. Ethics in the use of data, or data ethics, permeates research and business, requiring debate and training.\n\n\nThis presentation examines the goods promoted by the European Union’s Regulation 2016/679 (the General Data Protection Regulation [GDPR]) and the privacy the GDPR intends to protect. It looks at the role of personal data in the EU in terms of citizens, economics, and politics. It asks the question ‘Wherefore the public good in the EU law and legislation on data and AI?’ The role of the uses of data for health and science are examined against the background of the GDPR and the foreground of Open Science.\n\nThe views presented here are those of the speaker’s. They do not necessarily reflect those of any organisation. Francis gratefully acknowledges the work of the membership of the International Science Council’s CODATA International Data Policy Committee (IDPC), the RDA COVID-19 Working Group, and the EOSC-Future / RDA Artificial Intelligence & Data Visitation Working Group (AIDV-WG). Francis’ EOSC Future / RDA Ambassador for Ethics & Law and the EOSC-Future projects are co-funded by the European Union’s Horizon Programme call INFRAEOSC-03-2020 - Project ID 101017536. CODATA is funded by the International Science Council (ISC) and Horizon Europe as well as by other funding organisations. The Research Data Alliance (RDA) is funded by Horizon Europe as well as by other funding organisations (see here). Francis is grateful for the support provided by CODATA and the EOSC-Future and the RDA communities, structures, and secretariats.	eng	publication-presentation	info:eu-repo/semantics/presentation		\N	\N	\N	\N
CON_0113	PID_0085	rda_graph:PID_0085	Pertinent and Persistent Identifiers – a National Strategy		https://zenodo.org/records/4707935		https://www.rd-alliance.org/rdas-17th-plenary-meeting-posters-0	https://archive.rd-alliance.org/rdas-17th-plenary-meeting-posters-0	DOI	https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4707935	2021-04-21	Over the past year Jisc has led a project examining the role 5 key persistent identifiers (PIDs) can play in the open access landscape.\n\nThe priority PIDs are: outputs (DOI), people (ORCID), organisations (ROR), grants (DOI), projects (RAiD)\n\nWe present an overview of those PIDs along with a discussion of how embedding them within the wider open research workflows will have a positive impact on the FAIRness of data and metadata.\n\nFindable: Persistently identifying this information will protect the integrity of research data – ensuring correct attribution and accountability as well as enabling recognition of more contributions and roles for researchers allowing provenance to be established where lesser-known outputs are the basis for more established later “keystone” work.\n\nAccessible: The broadening of access to open science to more and diverse stakeholders by ensuring that within data and the associated works, the contributions of researchers, their funding, organisations and the narrative of the projects being undertaken are described with open metadata using agreed standards, which increases accessibility and addresses inequality.\n\nInteroperable: Machine to machine processes enabled by the metadata and infrastructure that support and enable PIDs decrease costs – financial, attentional and opportunity. That cost reduction also frees up time and generates more value-additive qualitative, narrative and / or contextual information\n\nReusable: Links expressed in the metadata associated with PIDs within, and created by, the entities (software, instruments etc.) involved in the emitting and processing of data enables easier reuse.	eng	publication-poster	info:eu-repo/semantics/poster		\N	\N	\N	\N
CON_0115	PID_0086	rda_graph:PID_0086	PIDs: Joining up the World - PID IG at RDA P16 2020		https://zenodo.org/records/4291878		https://www.rd-alliance.org/group/pid-ig/post/pids-joining-world-proposed-p16-session	https://archive.rd-alliance.org/group/pid-ig/post/pids-joining-world-proposed-p16-session	DOI	https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4291878	2020-11-26	The pervasive use of persistent identifiers (PIDs) is seen as a foundational element in anchoring and referencing research data in an interoperable way. The purpose of the Research Data Alliance (RDA) Persistent Identifier Interest Group (PID IG) is to synchronize identifier-related efforts, address important and emerging PID-related topics and coordinate activities, including appropriate RDA Working Groups, to practically solve PID-related issues from the engaged communities.\n\nThese presentations at RDA Plenary 16 (November 2020) were all about joining up the research world with PIDs and joining up the PID world with collaborative initiatives.\n\nUsing persistent identifiers to reference all the key elements of research is an aspiration to improve research efficiency and innovation in a globally joined-up information system. This session reviewed some of the latest developments in persistent identifiers for the scholarly system and identify opportunities for coordination.	eng	publication-presentation	info:eu-repo/semantics/presentation		\N	\N	\N	\N
CON_0116	PID_0087	rda_graph:PID_0087	Power and Persistent Identifiers		https://zenodo.org/records/2560791				DOI	https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.2560791	2018-11-23	Persistent identifiers are critical components of modern and evolving data infrastructure. Like much of the mechanics of civilization, PIDs are based on trusted registries. The operation of these registries can have significant power over how and when data are shared. This paper explores those power dynamics and suggests ways to balance the trade-offs between necessary control and maximizing the generative value of data.\n\nPresented at International Data Week 2018, 6 November 2018, Gaborone, Botswana.	eng	publication-presentation	info:eu-repo/semantics/presentation		\N	\N	\N	\N
CON_0119	PID_0090	rda_graph:PID_0090	Precisely and Persistently Identifying and Citing Arbitrary Subsets of Dynamic Data		https://zenodo.org/records/4571616		https://www.rd-alliance.org/harvard-data-science-review-features-paper-jointly-written-rda-dynamic-data-citation-working-group	https://archive.rd-alliance.org/harvard-data-science-review-features-paper-jointly-written-rda-dynamic-data-citation-working-group	DOI	https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4571616	2021-03-03	Precisely identifying arbitrary subsets of data so that these can be re-produced is a daunting challenge in data-driven science, the more so if the underlying data source is dynamically evolving. Yet, most settings exhibit exactly those characteristics: increasingly larger amounts of data being continuously ingested from a range of sources, with error correction and quality improvement processes adding to the dynamics. Yet, for studies to be reproducible, for decision-making to be transparent, and for meta studies to be performed conveniently, having a precise identification mechanism to reference, retrieve and work with such data is essential. The RDA Working Group on Dynamic Data Citation has published 14 recommendations that are centered around time- stamping and versioning evolving data sources and identifying subsets dynamically via persistent identifiers that are assigned to the queries selecting the respective subsets. These principles are generic and work for virtually any kind of data. In the past few years numerous repositories around the globe have implemented these recommendations and deployed solution. This paper provides an overview of the recommendations, reference implementations and pilot systems deployed and analyses key lessons learned from these. This provides a solid basis for institutions and researchers considering adding this functionality to their data infrastructure.	eng	publication-article	info:eu-repo/semantics/article		\N	\N	\N	\N
CON_0121	PID_0092	rda_graph:PID_0092	presentations from the RDA17 BoF on "Addressing the Challenges of Physical Data Access to Accelerate Science"		https://zenodo.org/records/4708071				DOI	https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4708071	2021-04-21	presentations from the RDA17 BoF on "Addressing the Challenges of Physical Data Access to Accelerate Science":\n\n \n\nhttps://www.rd-alliance.org/addressing-challenges-physical-data-access-accelerate-science-repositories-networks-and-petabytes	eng	publication-presentation	info:eu-repo/semantics/presentation		\N	\N	\N	\N
CON_0122	PID_0093	rda_graph:PID_0093	Principles and best practices in data versioning for all datasets big  and small		https://zenodo.org/records/3772870		https://www.rd-alliance.org/group_output/principles-and-best-practices-in-data-versioning-for-all-data-sets-big-and-small/		DOI	https://doi.org/10.15497/RDA00042	2020-04-06	The demand for better reproducibility of research results is growing. With more data becoming available online, it will become increasingly important for a researcher to be able to cite the exact extract of the dataset that was used to underpin their research publication. However, while the means to identify datasets using persistent identifiers have been in place for more than a decade, systematic data versioning practices are currently not available. Without these, it is very hard for researchers to gain attribution and credit for their actual contributions to the collection, creation and publishing of individual datasets. Versioning procedures and best practices are well established for scientific software and can be used to enable reproducibility of scientific results.\n\nThe Research Data Alliance (RDA) Data Versioning Working Group produced this Final Report to document 39 use cases and current practices, and to make recommendations for the versioning of research data. To further adoption of the outcomes, the Data Versioning Working Group then contributed selected use cases and recommended data versioning practices to other groups in RDA and W3C. The outcomes of the RDA Data Versioning Working Group add a central element to the systematic management of research data at any scale by providing recommendations for standard practices in the versioning of research data.\n\nThis revised report incorporates the feedback to the version 1.0 received from the RDA community review.	eng	publication-other	info:eu-repo/semantics/other	https://www.rd-alliance.org/sites/default/files/Principles%20and%20Best%20Practices%20in%20Data%20Versioning.pdf	\N	\N	\N	\N
