CON_0170	PID_0130	rda_graph:PID_0130	Recommendations for a minimal metadata set to aid harmonised discovery of learning resources		https://zenodo.org/records/6769695		https://www.rd-alliance.org/group_output/recommendations-for-a-minimal-metadata-set-to-aid-harmonised-discovery-of-learning-resources/		DOI	https://doi.org/10.15497/RDA00073	2022-06-09	As part of the Education And Training On Handling Of Research Data IG activities, the Minimal Metadata for Learning Resources Focus Group recommends a minimal set of metadata for learning resources. By comparing and analyzing existing learning resource-related metadata schemas to find the overlaps, the group provides guidance on metadata elements that should be minimally required for purposes of learning resource discovery to those concerned with supporting or providing training resources.  This set includes a report, a list of minimal metadata elements along with a data dictionary with examples for how to use the elements, and supporting documents.	eng	publication-other	info:eu-repo/semantics/other		\N	\N	\N	\N
CON_0171	PID_0131	rda_graph:PID_0131	REPOPSI Preliminary Start-Project Evaluation Using the CoreTrustSeal Requirements 2023-2025		https://zenodo.org/records/7535053				DOI	https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7535053	2022-12-26	We used the CoreTrustSeal Trustworthy Digital Repositories Requirements v01.00 2023-2025 (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7051011) to evaluate the Repository of psychological instruments in Serbian (REPOPSI) at the start of the project titled "TRUST-ification and FAIR-ification of an open repository for research instruments in psychology: REPOPSI's adoption of RDA outputs" (project team members: Aleksandra Lazić, Ljiljana Lazarević, Danka Purić, Iris Žeželj, Obrad Vučkovac).\n\nREPOPSI is an open repository for psychological measures, scales, tests, and other research instruments in Serbian. It is maintained by researchers at the Laboratory for Research of Individual Differences (LIRA) at the Faculty of Philosophy, University of Belgrade. REPOPSI is hosted on the Open Science Framework at https://osf.io/5zb8p/. \n\nThis record contains the checklist  (XSLX and CSV) which is the result of a preliminary self-assessment test using the CoreTrustSeal Trustworthy Digital Repositories Requirements v01.00 2023-2025. We indicated the compliance level for each of the Requirements on a binary scale (0 – In Progress: the repository is in the implementation phase; 1 – Implemented: the requirement has been fully implemented by the repository). To be able to measure REPOPSI's progress, we indicated the compliance level on a continuous scale as well (0 – Not applicable; 1 – The repository has not considered this yet; 2 – The repository has a theoretical concept; 3 – The repository is in the implementation phase; 4 – The guideline has been fully implemented in the repository). This scale used to be applied according to the CoreTrustSeal Trustworthy Data Repositories Requirements 2020-2022 (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.3632533).\n\nThis project has received funding from the European Union's Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme under grant agreement No 101017536 and is supported by the EOSC Future (https://eoscfuture.eu/) through the RDA (https://www.rd-alliance.org/) Open Call mechanism (https://eoscfuture-grants.eu/provider/research-data-alliance), based on evaluations of external, independent experts.	eng	publication-report	info:eu-repo/semantics/report		\N	\N	\N	\N
CON_0172	PID_0132	rda_graph:PID_0132	REPOPSI Start-Project Evaluation Using the RDA FAIR Data Maturity Model		https://zenodo.org/records/7535024				DOI	https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7535024	2022-12-26	We used the FAIR Data Maturity Model to evaluate the Repository of psychological instruments in Serbian (REPOPSI) at the start of the project titled "TRUST-ification and FAIR-ification of an open repository for research instruments in psychology: REPOPSI's adoption of RDA outputs" (project team members: Aleksandra Lazić, Ljiljana Lazarević, Danka Purić, Iris Žeželj, Obrad Vučkovac).\n\nREPOPSI is an open repository for psychological measures, scales, tests, and other research instruments in Serbian. It is maintained by researchers at the Laboratory for Research of Individual Differences (LIRA) at the Faculty of Philosophy, University of Belgrade. REPOPSI is hosted on the Open Science Framework at https://osf.io/5zb8p/. \n\nWe evaluated REPOPSI's Findability, Accessibility, Interoperability, and Reusability (i.e. FAIR-ness) using the "FAIR Data Maturity Model: Specification and Guidelines (1.0)" document (https://doi.org/10.15497/rda00050) developed by the RDA FAIR Data Maturity Model Working Group. We measured the progress per indicator, on a continuous scale (0 – not applicable; 1 – not being considered yet; 2 – under consideration or in planning phase; 3 – in implementation phase; 4 – fully implemented).\n\nThis record contains the checklist (XSLX and CSV) and the report (PDF), which describes the reasoning behind the determined maturity level of each indicator as well as the visualization of the evaluation results (radar charts).\n\nThis project has received funding from the European Union's Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme under grant agreement No 101017536 and is supported by the EOSC Future (https://eoscfuture.eu/) through the RDA (https://www.rd-alliance.org/) Open Call mechanism (https://eoscfuture-grants.eu/provider/research-data-alliance), based on evaluations of external, independent experts.	eng	publication-report	info:eu-repo/semantics/report		\N	\N	\N	\N
CON_0174	PID_0134	rda_graph:PID_0134	REPORT on RDA EUROPE 4.0 AMBASSADOR PROGRAMME		https://zenodo.org/records/4707740				DOI	https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4707740	2020-09-29	The Ambassador programme set up by the RDA Europe 4.0 project can be seen as a pilot. This report aims at informing RDA Global and the RDA Europe 4.0 project about the outcomes of the programme. \n\nBased on interviews with the Ambassadors, it summarises their motivations, how they set up their work programme, the activities they performed, the challenges they met, and their messages to the RDA, which provide useful feedback and input at different levels.\n\nConclusions are drawn on the Ambassadors’ role, possible next steps, the value of the ambassadorship for the Ambassadors, and the value of the Ambassadors’ programme for the RDA.\n\nTaglines extracted from the interviews which could be reused when describing the RDA value are listed, as well as practical recommendations for similar programmes which may be set up.	eng	publication-report	info:eu-repo/semantics/report		\N	\N	\N	\N
CON_0175	PID_0135	rda_graph:PID_0135	Report on RDA integration with other European initiatives		https://zenodo.org/records/4518847				DOI	https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4518847	2020-01-27	This report describes how RDA Europe 4.0 supports the integration of RDA in the broader landscape of related European initiatives, with a focus on projects and activities implementing the European Open Science Cloud (EOSC).\nThe report begins by positioning RDA in relation to the European Open Science Cloud.\n\nThis is followed by an in-depth analysis of how RDA contributes to EOSC-related projects based on a survey completed by 22 projects. The survey demonstrates how the RDA platform, community and outputs are being leveraged to deliver project objectives and also shows how work conducted within RDA is being extended to meet the needs of EOSC projects and user communities. The report continues with examples of RDA groups that facilitate collaboration between EOSC projects. This section concludes with a description of workshops co-organised by RDA Europe 4.0 with EOSC projects identified of particular relevance to RDA: namely, OpenAIRE, EOSC-hub, FAIRsFAIR and FREYA.\n\nLater sections describe collaborative activities undertaken by communities and individuals supported by RDA Europe 4.0: the Group of European Data Experts in RDA (GEDE-RDA), the RDA Europe Ambassadors, and the RDA Europe national nodes.	eng	publication-projectDeliverable	info:eu-repo/semantics/projectDeliverable		\N	\N	\N	\N
